+# Methodology
+
+What a good workout is, for the planner to judge against. Written by a human (TRAINER.md § Stores); the engine never checks it (ADR-0001).
+
+> **Draft.** Written by the agent for the user to edit. Every line is either **[lit]**, resting on a source below, or **[decide]**, a choice that is the user's and is only proposed here. Nothing here is the user's yet.
+
+These are criteria, not rules. A plan that departs from one is not refused; it says why in the block's `reason`.
+
+## 1. Every block has an intent, and the intent sets the rest
+
+| intent | reps per set | effort | rest after a round | |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| strength | 3-6 | 1-3 reps short of failure | 2-3 min | [lit] Grgic 2018, ACSM 2009 |
+| hypertrophy, compound | 6-15 | 0-3 reps short of failure | 2-3 min | [lit] Schoenfeld 2016, Grgic 2017 |
+| hypertrophy, isolation | 10-20 | 0-2 reps short of failure | 60-90 s | [lit] Grgic 2017; [decide] the band |
+| muscular endurance / conditioning | 15+ or timed | hard, not to failure | 30-60 s | [lit] ACSM 2009 |
+
+- **Hypertrophy grows across a wide rep range if the set ends near failure.** Load matters little between about 6 and 30 reps. [lit] Schoenfeld 2017b, Lopez 2021
+- **Longer rest buys more reps on the next set**, and so more volume. Short rest is a time saving paid for in work. [lit] Schoenfeld 2016
+- The engine's bands (90-120 s for hypertrophy) sit below what the literature favours for compound work. [decide] keep them, or move them to 2-3 min.
+
+## 2. A working set is a set close to failure
+
+- **A working set ends 0-3 reps short of failure.** A set that ends far from it counts for little, whatever the reps. [lit] Refalo 2023
+- **With bodyweight and bands, the variant is the load.** Pick the variant or band that puts failure inside the target reps: decline or deficit push-ups when 15 flat ones are easy, a heavier band when 20 are. [decide] the reps at which you move to a harder variant.
+- **Variants are a progression, not a volume.** Four push-up variants back to back are four sets of one movement, not four exercises.
+
+## 3. Volume: sets before exercises
+
+- **Hard sets per muscle per week**: about 10 or more for growth, with returns diminishing past roughly 20. [lit] Schoenfeld 2017a, Schoenfeld 2019
+- **Training a muscle twice a week beats once**, at equal volume or close to it. [lit] Schoenfeld 2016b
+- **Per group in one workout**: [decide] proposed 1-2 exercises and 6-10 working sets for a group with half the workout, 1 exercise and 3-4 sets for a group with less.
+- **A second exercise for a group has to add something**: a different angle, a different joint action, a different muscle of the group. Arms are biceps and triceps; two triceps exercises and no biceps is one muscle twice. [decide]
+- **When time is left, add sets to the work that matters before adding an exercise.** A tenth exercise in a circuit is the cheap way to fill a budget, not a better workout. [decide]
+
+## 4. Structure: when a superset or a circuit is the right answer
+
+| structure | right when | wrong when | |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **straight sets** | the main work of the day; strength; anything where the next set's reps matter | never wrong, only slower | [lit] ACSM 2009 |
+| **superset, antagonists** (push with pull, biceps with triceps) | saving time with little loss: each muscle rests while the other works | the two compete for the same muscle or the same grip | [lit] Robbins 2010, Weakley 2017 |
+| **superset, same muscle** | a deliberate finisher for hypertrophy, at the end | as the main work: the second exercise loses reps | [lit] Weakley 2017 |
+| **circuit** | conditioning; a short reduced workout; low-skill exercises for different muscles | its exercises train the same muscle: that is one long set with no rest, not rounds | [decide] |
+
+- **Rest belongs to the muscle, not the block.** In a superset of antagonists a muscle rests for the other exercise plus the block's rest, so the block's rest can be shorter.
+- **Order**: the most demanding and most compound first, the group the day is for first, isolation and finishers last. [lit] ACSM 2009
+
+## 5. The plan that started this, read against the above
+
+`Blocco 1`: four push-up variants and a band fly in one circuit, 8 reps each, 90 s after the round.
+
+- One movement, five times, no rest between: one long set, not three rounds of five exercises (§ 4).
+- 8 reps of an incline push-up is far from failure for most people: not working sets (§ 2).
+- 25 of 45 minutes, with the time that was left spent on nothing (§ 3).
+
+## Open questions for the user
+
+1. Your goal for now: strength, size, or staying consistent. It decides which row of § 1 is the default.
+2. The rest bands: keep the engine's, or the literature's (§ 1)?
+3. How close to failure you want to train, and whether that changes on bodyweight work.
+4. Sets per group per workout (§ 3).
+5. Whether a circuit ever belongs in a full workout, or only in a reduced one (§ 4).
+6. Anything you do that this leaves out: warm-up, the order you like, exercises you always keep.
+
